MIY = "This lesson summarises ideas from The Mountain Is You by Brianna Wiest in our own words."
BTB = "This lesson summarises ideas from Better Than Before by Gretchen Rubin in our own words."
L = {}
L['miy-triggers'] = dict(
intro="A trigger is a moment when a small event provokes a reaction much bigger than the situation seems to call for. A casual comment stings for days, a delayed reply sparks panic, a certain tone of voice makes us shut down. The Mountain Is You suggests that, uncomfortable as they are, triggers can be signposts pointing towards what needs care. This lesson explores how to read them gently. " + MIY,
sections=[
("What a trigger is", "Everyday language often uses 'triggered' loosely, but the idea here is specific: a reaction that feels out of proportion to the present moment. If a friend forgetting your birthday leaves you mildly disappointed, that is an ordinary response. If it leaves you feeling worthless for a week, something deeper may be involved. The intensity is the clue. It suggests that the present event has touched an older feeling or belief."),
("Triggers as information", "The book's view is that triggers show us where we have unresolved feelings or unmet needs. Rather than treating them only as problems to avoid, we can treat them as information. A strong reaction to criticism might point to a long-held fear of not being good enough. Strong jealousy might point to something you deeply want but have not allowed yourself to pursue. In this way, the trigger becomes a guide rather than just an enemy."),
("Pausing before reacting", "When a trigger hits, the urge is often to react immediately: to snap back, withdraw, or spiral into worry. A small pause can make a big difference. Taking a few slow breaths, naming the feeling silently ('this is hurt' or 'this is fear'), or stepping away for a moment gives space between the trigger and the response. In that space, you can choose a response that fits your values rather than the old pattern."),
("Looking underneath", "Later, when you are calmer, it can help to reflect: What exactly set this off? What did it remind me of? What did I believe about myself in that moment? Writing the answers down can make patterns clearer over time. You may notice that certain kinds of situations reliably stir the same feelings, and that awareness alone often softens their power."),
("Gentleness and support", "Some triggers are connected to painful past experiences. Exploring them can stir up difficult feelings. Go at your own pace, and if a trigger feels overwhelming or connected to trauma, a qualified professional can help you work through it safely. Self-reflection is useful, but it is not a substitute for care when you need it."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the 'seen' message", "Nisha, a marketing executive in Mumbai, noticed that when friends saw her messages without replying, she felt a wave of panic and spent hours rereading what she had written. One evening, instead of spiralling, she paused and wrote down what she felt: 'I'm afraid they're annoyed with me and will leave.' She realised this fear was familiar from school years when she often felt left out. Naming it did not erase it, but the next time it happened, she recognised the old feeling and reminded herself that a late reply usually just means a busy friend. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["A trigger is a reaction that feels bigger than the present moment calls for.","The intensity of a reaction can point to older feelings or needs.","Pause, breathe and name the feeling before responding.","Reflect later on patterns; seek professional support if triggers feel overwhelming."],
reflect=["What situation reliably produces a stronger reaction in you than you would like?","If that reaction could speak, what might it be trying to protect?"],
)
L['miy-eq'] = dict(
intro="Emotional intelligence is the ability to notice, understand and work with emotions, both your own and other people's. The Mountain Is You treats building emotional intelligence as a key part of moving past self-sabotage. This lesson explores what that looks like in daily life, in plain language. " + MIY,
sections=[
("Feelings are not the enemy", "Many of us grow up learning, directly or indirectly, that certain feelings are bad: anger is rude, sadness is weakness, fear is shameful. As a result, we may push feelings away or pretend they are not there. The book suggests that emotions carry useful information about our needs and boundaries. Anger can signal that a line has been crossed. Sadness can signal loss. Anxiety can signal that something matters to us. Ignoring them does not make them go away; it often makes them louder."),
("Feeling without being ruled", "Emotional intelligence does not mean suppressing feelings, and it does not mean acting on every feeling either. It means allowing yourself to feel something fully while still choosing how to respond. You can feel angry and decide not to shout. You can feel afraid and still take a careful step forward. Learning to hold a feeling without being swept away by it is a skill that develops with practice."),
("Naming emotions", "A simple but powerful habit is putting feelings into words. 'I feel bad' is vague. 'I feel disappointed and a bit embarrassed' is much clearer. A richer vocabulary for emotions makes it easier to understand what you need. Some people find it helpful to check in with themselves a few times a day: What am I feeling right now? Where do I notice it in my body?"),
("Understanding others", "Emotional intelligence also includes noticing what others may be feeling. A colleague who snaps might be under pressure. A friend who goes quiet might be hurting. Being curious instead of assuming the worst, and asking gently ('You seem a bit low today, is everything okay?'), can strengthen relationships and reduce conflict."),
("Practising patience with yourself", "Building emotional intelligence is a gradual process, and everyone has days when old reactions take over. That is part of learning. Each time you notice a feeling, name it, or pause before reacting, you are strengthening the skill, even if the next moment does not go perfectly."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the tense dinner", "At a family dinner in Patna, Aditya's father criticised his choice of career in front of relatives. Aditya felt his face go hot and wanted to argue loudly. Instead, he took a breath and silently named what he felt: 'hurt and embarrassed'. He said calmly, 'I'd like to talk about this later, just us two.' Later that evening, they spoke privately, and Aditya was able to explain his plans without the heat of the moment. The feeling had not vanished, but he had chosen how to respond. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Emotions carry useful information about needs and boundaries.","Emotional intelligence means feeling fully while choosing how to respond.","Naming emotions precisely makes them easier to understand.","Curiosity about others' feelings can strengthen relationships."],
reflect=["Which emotion do you find hardest to allow yourself to feel?","When did you last respond to a strong feeling in a way you were proud of?"],
)
L['miy-future'] = dict(
intro="It is easy to imagine a better future and much harder to live in a way that leads there. The Mountain Is You ends by encouraging readers to start building a new future through small, deliberate choices today, guided by the person they want to become. This lesson explores how that idea can work in everyday life. " + MIY,
sections=[
("Imagining your future self", "The book invites readers to picture the person they hope to become: how they spend their days, how they treat themselves and others, what they value. This is not about fantasy or perfection. It is about getting clear enough on a direction that daily choices can be measured against it. A clear picture makes it easier to notice when you are drifting away from it."),
("Acting before you feel ready", "A common trap is waiting to feel confident, motivated or ready before acting. The book suggests that these feelings often come after action, not before. If you wait to feel like a disciplined person before acting like one, you may wait a long time. Taking small actions consistent with your future self, even when you do not feel ready, can gradually build the confidence you were waiting for."),
("Letting go of the old self", "Moving towards a new future can involve grief for the old one. Familiar habits, relationships or self-images may no longer fit. The book acknowledges that this can feel uncomfortable and even lonely. It is normal to feel a pull back towards the old patterns. Recognising that discomfort as part of growth, rather than as a sign that something is wrong, can make it easier to keep going."),
("Daily choices as building blocks", "The future is built mostly from ordinary days. How you start the morning, how you respond to stress, what you do with free time: these small choices add up. Asking 'What would my future self do here?' at a few key moments each day can gently steer your path. You do not need to get every choice right. You just need to keep returning to the direction you chose."),
("Meaning and purpose", "Building a future is not only about productivity or achievement. The book connects it to living in line with your values and finding meaning. A future self might be calmer, kinder, more present with family, or more creative, not only more successful. Choosing what matters to you, rather than what others expect, makes the journey more worthwhile."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the future-self question", "Shreya, a mid-career accountant in Coimbatore, often imagined herself as someone who painted again, as she had in college. She kept waiting for a free month that never came. She started asking herself each evening, 'What would the painter version of me do with thirty spare minutes?' Some evenings the answer was 'sketch for ten minutes'. Over a few months, she had filled a sketchbook and felt more like herself than she had in years. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Picture the person you want to become as a guide for daily choices.","Confidence often follows action, not the other way round.","Letting go of old patterns can feel uncomfortable; that is part of growth.","Build the future through ordinary daily choices aligned with your values."],
reflect=["Describe one day in the life of the person you hope to become.","What is one small action that version of you would take tomorrow?"],
)
L['btb-tendencies'] = dict(
intro="Why do some people keep every promise to others but struggle with promises to themselves? Why do some resist any rule they are given? Better Than Before introduces a framework called the Four Tendencies, which describes how people respond to expectations. This lesson explains the framework and how it can help you design habits that suit you. " + BTB,
sections=[
("Two kinds of expectations", "The framework starts with two kinds of expectations. Outer expectations come from others: a work deadline, a request from a friend, a doctor's suggestion. Inner expectations come from yourself: a resolution to read more, a plan to start exercising. The book observes that people differ in how they respond to each kind, and that this difference shapes how habits work for them."),
("The four tendencies", "The book describes four patterns. Upholders tend to meet both outer and inner expectations readily; they like rules and plans. Questioners tend to meet expectations only when they understand and agree with the reasons; they turn outer expectations into inner ones. Obligers tend to meet outer expectations reliably but struggle with inner ones; they keep promises to others more easily than to themselves. Rebels tend to resist expectations of both kinds and prefer to act from choice and freedom."),
("Why this matters for habits", "Because people respond differently, the same habit strategy will not suit everyone. An Upholder may do well with a simple schedule. A Questioner may need to research why a habit is worthwhile before committing. An Obliger may benefit most from outer accountability, such as a walking partner or a class. A Rebel may do better framing a habit as a choice that expresses who they are, rather than a rule they must obey."),
("Using the framework wisely", "The tendencies are a tool for self-understanding, not a fixed label or a personality test with a score. Many people recognise themselves strongly in one, while others see a mix. The value lies in asking: 'What kind of approach actually works for me?' rather than copying someone else's routine and feeling like a failure when it does not fit."),
("Understanding others", "The framework can also help in relationships and at work. If a colleague keeps asking 'why', they may simply need reasons, not be difficult. If a family member resists being told what to do, offering choices may work better than instructions. Seeing these differences can reduce frustration on both sides."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: the yoga class", "Divya, an IT professional in Noida, had tried many times to do yoga at home using videos and always stopped after a few days. When she read about the Four Tendencies, she recognised herself as likely an Obliger: she never missed a meeting at work, but her own plans slipped. She joined a small morning class where the teacher expected her, and a friend came along too. With outer accountability in place, she attended regularly for months. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["People respond differently to outer and inner expectations.","The four patterns are Upholder, Questioner, Obliger and Rebel.","Choose habit strategies that suit how you respond to expectations.","Use the framework for understanding, not as a fixed label."],
reflect=["Do you find it easier to keep promises to others or to yourself?","Which habit strategy has failed for you, and might it have suited a different tendency?"],
)
L['btb-foundation'] = dict(
intro="When life feels chaotic, it can be hard to change anything. Better Than Before describes a Strategy of Foundation: focusing first on habits that strengthen self-command and make everything else easier. This lesson explores what foundation habits are and why they deserve attention. " + BTB,
sections=[
("What foundation habits are", "The book identifies certain areas that tend to support all other habits. Broadly, these are sleeping, moving, eating and drinking wisely, and keeping your surroundings in order. When these areas are neglected, people often feel tired, irritable or scattered, and other habits become much harder to keep. When they are reasonably steady, there is more energy and patience available for everything else."),
("Sleep", "Sleep is often the first thing sacrificed when time is short. Yet tiredness can make it harder to resist temptation, focus or stay patient. Small steps, such as going to bed a little earlier or keeping screens out of bed, can make a noticeable difference for many people. Everyone's sleep needs differ; if you have persistent sleep problems, a qualified professional can help."),
("Movement", "Movement does not have to mean intense exercise. Walking, stretching, climbing stairs or dancing in the kitchen all count. Many people find that some daily movement lifts their mood and energy. The aim is regular, enjoyable movement that suits your body and life, not a punishing routine."),
("Eating and drinking", "Eating regular meals and drinking enough water can steady energy across the day. The book's interest here is not in a particular diet but in avoiding the energy crashes and irritability that can come from skipping meals or relying heavily on quick snacks. This lesson does not offer dietary advice; for specific needs, consult a qualified professional."),
("Order in your surroundings", "A cluttered, chaotic space can make it harder to think clearly. The book suggests that bringing some outer order, such as making the bed or clearing a desk, can contribute to a sense of inner calm. It does not have to be perfect; even small areas of order can help. Together, these four foundations create a base on which other habits can grow more easily."),
],
example=("Illustrative example: fixing sleep first", "Ravi, a sales manager in Hyderabad, wanted to start studying for a certification, exercise more and spend more time with his kids. He kept failing at all three. Looking at his days, he realised he was sleeping only a few hours most nights because he scrolled his phone until late. He decided to work on just one thing first: phone off and charging outside the bedroom by eleven. After a few weeks of better rest, he found it much easier to wake up for a morning walk and stay patient in the evenings. This is an illustrative story."),
takeaways=["Foundation habits support all other habits.","The foundations are sleep, movement, eating and drinking wisely, and outer order.","Strengthening one foundation can make other changes easier.","This is general learning; consult a professional for health-specific needs."],
reflect=["Which foundation, sleep, movement, food or order, feels weakest for you right now?","How might improving it make another habit easier?"],
)
